Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that best characterizes the agreement's requirements.

Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: Section 9.3 Superior Proposal Evaluation. Notwithstanding any other provisions in this Agreement, the Merger Advisory Committee (MAC) shall evaluate any Acquisition Proposal to determine whether it qualifies as a Superior Proposal. To do so, the MAC must satisfy all of the following conditions: 
(i) After independent consultations with external legal counsel and an independent financial analyst, the MAC must endorse that the proposal's financial components meet minimum thresholds; 
(ii) The proposal must incorporate significant non-financial benefits, including strategic partnerships, market synergy effects, or regulatory advantages, which are essential for long-term viability; 
(iii) Sole amendments affecting exclusively the financial terms shall not, absent any accompanying qualitative changes, convert a proposal into a Superior Proposal; 
(iv) Even in cases where the proposal appears attractive on financial terms, if the evaluation is based solely on these metrics, the MAC is mandated to submit the proposal for a secondary review by its Special Review Subcommittee to verify its compliance with fiduciary obligations. In effect, the comprehensive evaluation mandates an integration of both financial and non-financial factors.

